{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":6216,"ns":0,"title":"Chinese room","extract":"The Chinese room argument holds that a computer executing a program cannot have a mind, understanding, or consciousness, regardless of how intelligently or human-like the program may make the computer behave. The argument was presented in a 1980 paper by the American philosopher John Searle, entitled \"Minds, Brains, and Programs\" and published in the journal Behavioral and Brain Sciences.","description":"Thought experiment on artificial intelligence","descriptionsource":"local"}]}}